  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nThe airmass across the area has destabilized this afternoon with ML \nCapes up into the 1500-2000 J/KG range. This, combined with weak mid\nlevel energy moving through the Ohio Valley, will lead to fairly \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms this afternoon into this \nevening. Effective bulk shear has nudged up into the 20-30 knot \nrange and DCAPEs are pushing up close to 1000 J/KG, so suppose a few\nstrong to possibly severe storms could be possible. The main threat \nwould be damaging winds this afternoon into early evening along with \nlocally heavy rainfall.\n\nThe broad upper level trough over the Great Lakes will begin to \ndeepen somewhat as we head into early to mid next week. With \ncontinued embedded rounds of mid level energy/weak short waves \nmoving through the trough, we will continue to see occasional \nchances for showers and thunderstorms at times through early to mid \nnext week. As the flow begins to gradually increase, there will be \nan uptick in deeper layer shear through this time period. As a \nresult, it looks like there could be a better severe threat early to\nmid next week. With a warm and moist airmass in place, PWs will \nremain relatively high, so locally heavy rainfall could lead to an \nincreased flooding threat through this period, especially in areas \nthat see repeated rounds of storms.\n\nSomewhat better northwest flow aloft will develop through the end of\nthe week. This should lead to a drier and cooler trend toward the \nend of the forecast period.",
